export const DATA_71: ReadingTest[] = [
  { id: "rd-104", num: 104, title: "Đề 104", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "Thanksgiving: History, Traditions, and Modern Celebrations", paragraphs: ["Every year on the fourth Thursday of November, millions of families across the United States gather around tables laden with roasted turkey, stuffing, cranberry sauce, and pumpkin pie to celebrate Thanksgiving — one of America's most beloved national holidays. The occasion brings together people of all backgrounds to share a meal, express gratitude for the good things in their lives, and enjoy time with family and friends. While Thanksgiving is often associated with a specific historical event, its meaning and traditions have evolved considerably over the centuries into something far richer and more complex than any single origin story can capture.", "The most commonly cited origin of Thanksgiving dates to the autumn of 1621, when the Pilgrim settlers of Plymouth Colony in present-day Massachusetts shared a three-day harvest feast with members of the Wampanoag people who had helped them survive their first brutal winter in the New World. The Pilgrims had arrived on the Mayflower the previous year with little knowledge of local farming conditions, and many had died from cold and disease during the winter months. The Wampanoag, led by a man named Squanto who had previously lived in England and spoke English, taught the settlers how to cultivate corn, fish the local waters, and avoid poisonous plants. The harvest feast of 1621 is widely regarded as a symbol of cooperation and mutual gratitude, though historians caution that the relationship between the Pilgrims and the Wampanoag was complex and that the event was not considered a formal holiday at the time.", "For many decades after 1621, individual colonies and states held their own thanksgiving observances on different dates and for different reasons, often in response to military victories or the end of droughts and other hardships. It was not until 1863 that President Abraham Lincoln, urged by writer and magazine editor Sarah Josepha Hale — who had campaigned for a national day of thanksgiving for nearly forty years — proclaimed Thanksgiving a national holiday to be observed on the last Thursday of November. Lincoln's proclamation came at a particularly difficult moment in American history, during the Civil War, and was intended to encourage unity and gratitude among a deeply divided nation. The date was later adjusted by President Franklin D. Roosevelt in 1939 and fixed to the fourth Thursday of November by an act of Congress in 1941.", "Today, Thanksgiving is celebrated with a rich array of food traditions that vary by region and family background. The turkey has become the undisputed centrepiece of the Thanksgiving meal, so central to the holiday that the day is often informally called \"Turkey Day.\" Alongside the turkey, traditional dishes typically include mashed potatoes, sweet potatoes, green bean casserole, macaroni and cheese, and a variety of pies, most notably pumpkin pie. Families often pass down recipes through generations, and the preparation of the meal is itself considered an important part of the celebration. In recent years, plant-based alternatives to turkey have become increasingly popular, reflecting changing dietary habits and growing awareness of environmental sustainability.", "Beyond the dinner table, Thanksgiving is associated with several other widely observed customs. The Macy's Thanksgiving Day Parade, held annually in New York City since 1924, features giant character balloons, floats, marching bands, and performances by popular entertainers, drawing millions of live spectators and television viewers each year. American football has also become deeply intertwined with the holiday, with the National Football League scheduling major games on Thanksgiving Day as a tradition dating back to the late nineteenth century. Many families also use the occasion to volunteer at food banks and community kitchens, feeding people who might otherwise go without a proper meal on the holiday.", "Not all Americans, however, view Thanksgiving with uncomplicated joy. For many Native American communities, the holiday is a painful reminder of the colonization, displacement, and cultural destruction that followed the early encounters between European settlers and Indigenous peoples. Since 1970, members of the Wampanoag Nation and their supporters have gathered at Plymouth Rock on Thanksgiving Day for the National Day of Mourning, a solemn ceremony of remembrance and protest. Their presence invites a more critical and complete reflection on the full history behind the holiday — one that sits alongside, rather than replaces, the tradition of gratitude and togetherness that millions of Americans continue to value each year."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of this passage?", options: ["Thanksgiving should be abolished because it misrepresents the true history of relations between European settlers and Native Americans", "Thanksgiving is a holiday with deep historical roots, rich food and cultural traditions, and a complex legacy that includes both gratitude and grief", "Thanksgiving is a uniquely American holiday whose traditions have remained unchanged since the original Pilgrim feast of 1621", "The most important aspect of Thanksgiving is the Macy's Thanksgiving Day Parade, which draws millions of viewers every year"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"laden\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Decorated attractively with flowers, candles, and seasonal ornaments for a special family occasion", "Surrounded by family members who have gathered from different parts of the country to celebrate", "Carefully arranged in a specific pattern to follow the customs of a traditional holiday meal", "Heavily loaded or covered with a large amount of something, especially food or goods"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, who played a key role in persuading President Lincoln to declare Thanksgiving a national holiday?", options: ["Sarah Josepha Hale, a writer and magazine editor who had campaigned for a national day of thanksgiving for nearly forty years", "Members of the Wampanoag Nation who had long advocated for official recognition of their role in the 1621 harvest feast", "President Franklin D. Roosevelt, who first proposed making Thanksgiving a fixed national holiday during the Great Depression", "A group of New England governors who jointly petitioned Congress to establish a unified national day of thanksgiving"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"them\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["The crew members of the Mayflower who remained in Plymouth Colony after the ship's return to England", "The members of the Wampanoag people who led by Squanto helped European newcomers in Massachusetts", "The children and elderly passengers who were most vulnerable to cold and disease during the winter months", "The Pilgrim settlers of Plymouth Colony who had arrived on the Mayflower and struggled to survive their first winter"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following best describes the Macy's Thanksgiving Day Parade?", options: ["An annual New York City event featuring giant balloons, floats, marching bands, and performances that attracts millions of viewers", "A community-organized march through city streets where participants donate food to local shelters and soup kitchens", "A religious procession held in Washington D.C. since 1863 to mark the official presidential proclamation of Thanksgiving", "A televised sporting event that combines a charity auction with a live musical concert at a major football stadium"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, why do some Native American communities view Thanksgiving with pain rather than joy?", options: ["Because the holiday commercializes their cultural heritage without providing any financial recognition or compensation", "Because the food traditions of Thanksgiving ignore the contribution of Indigenous peoples to the settlers' survival", "Because the Wampanoag Nation was not officially invited to participate in the 1621 harvest feast with the Pilgrim settlers", "Because the holiday reminds them of the colonization, displacement, and cultural destruction that followed early European settlement"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a Thanksgiving custom beyond the dinner table?", options: ["Volunteering at food banks and community kitchens to provide meals for people who cannot afford a holiday dinner", "Travelling to Plymouth, Massachusetts, to visit historical sites connected to the original 1621 Pilgrim harvest feast", "Watching or attending American football games scheduled by the National Football League on Thanksgiving Day", "Watching the Macy's Thanksgiving Day Parade either in person in New York City or on television at home"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"intertwined\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Gradually introduced to a new audience through repeated exposure in mainstream media over several decades", "Officially endorsed and financially sponsored by a national organization as part of a public awareness campaign", "Temporarily associated with a cultural event for promotional purposes during a specific calendar period each year", "So closely connected or linked with something that the two are difficult to think of or separate from each other"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, why have plant-based alternatives to turkey become more popular at Thanksgiving in recent years?", options: ["Because younger generations prefer the taste and texture of plant-based proteins over traditional roasted turkey", "Because government health campaigns have encouraged Americans to reduce their consumption of red and white meat", "Because turkey has become too expensive for many families due to rising food prices and supply chain disruptions", "Because changing dietary habits and growing awareness of environmental sustainability have influenced food choices"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What is the author's overall attitude toward Thanksgiving as expressed in this passage?", options: ["Purely celebratory, presenting Thanksgiving as an occasion of joy and unity that brings out the best in American society", "Balanced and informative, recognizing both the meaningful traditions of the holiday and the legitimate grievances it raises", "Strongly critical, arguing that the holiday should be reformed to focus entirely on acknowledging the suffering of Native Americans", "Indifferent and detached, treating Thanksgiving as a minor cultural custom with little significance for modern Americans"], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "Nanowood: A Sustainable Material Transforming Thermal Insulation", paragraphs: ["As global demand for energy-efficient buildings and sustainable materials intensifies, researchers are increasingly turning to nature for inspiration. One of the most promising recent developments in this field is nanowood — a lightweight, biodegradable material derived from natural wood through a process that strips away lignin and most of the cellulose, leaving behind an aligned nanoscale structure of extraordinary thermal and mechanical properties. First reported by a team at the University of Maryland in 2018, nanowood has since attracted significant attention from materials scientists, architects, and sustainability advocates who see in it the potential to revolutionize the insulation industry, which has long depended on fossil-fuel-derived products such as expanded polystyrene and polyurethane foam.", "The manufacturing process begins with natural wood, typically balsa wood, which is prized for its low density and highly aligned cellular structure. The wood is first treated with a solution of sodium hydroxide and sodium sulfite — a chemical bath that selectively removes the lignin responsible for wood's characteristic brown colour and much of its structural rigidity. This process, known as delignification , is followed by a cold-pressing and freeze-drying step that preserves the wood's natural channel-like cellular architecture in a highly compressed form. The result is a white, paper-thin material that retains the directional alignment of the original wood cells — a structural feature that proves critical to nanowood's exceptional insulating performance.", "Nanowood's thermal insulation capability derives directly from this aligned cellular architecture. When heat attempts to travel through the material in the direction perpendicular to the cell channels — that is, across the grain of the wood — it encounters a highly tortuous path filled with countless air-filled nanoscale pores that dramatically slow the conduction of thermal energy. [1] Laboratory measurements have confirmed that nanowood achieves a thermal conductivity of approximately 2.7 milliwatts per metre-kelvin in this direction — significantly lower than that of expanded polystyrene, one of the most widely used commercial insulation materials. [2] In the parallel direction, along the grain, thermal conductivity is considerably higher, meaning nanowood is an anisotropic insulator: its performance varies depending on the direction of heat flow. [3] This directionality, while a potential limitation in certain applications, can also be exploited as an advantage in building designs that require selective heat channelling or cooling. [4]", "Beyond its thermal properties, nanowood offers a range of additional characteristics that distinguish it from conventional synthetic insulation materials. Its specific strength — the ratio of mechanical strength to weight — rivals that of many engineering plastics and metals, making it capable of bearing structural loads that polystyrene foam could never support. Its optical properties are also notable: when illuminated, nanowood scatters light across the visible spectrum, producing a bright white appearance that gives it potential applications as a sustainable alternative to synthetic white pigments in coatings and paints. Additionally, because nanowood is derived entirely from a natural, renewable source and is fully biodegradable, it avoids the end-of-life disposal problems associated with synthetic insulation materials, which often release microplastics or toxic gases when incinerated or landfilled.", "The environmental credentials of nanowood extend to its production process as well. The chemicals used in delignification — sodium hydroxide and sodium sulfite — are both recyclable within a closed-loop industrial system, reducing the waste and chemical pollution typically associated with materials manufacturing. The use of balsa wood as the primary feedstock raises some sustainability questions, since balsa is primarily grown in Ecuador and its increasing commercial demand has been linked to deforestation concerns in some regions. However, researchers have demonstrated that nanowood can be produced from a wide variety of wood species, including fast-growing trees such as basswood, pine, and poplar, which can be sustainably harvested from managed forests with minimal ecological impact.", "Despite its impressive properties, nanowood faces several practical challenges on the path to widespread commercial adoption. The freeze-drying step in its production is energy-intensive and time-consuming, adding to manufacturing costs that currently make nanowood considerably more expensive than conventional insulation materials. The material's sensitivity to moisture is another concern: although its cellulose structure provides inherent strength, prolonged exposure to high humidity can compromise its mechanical performance and insulating efficiency. Researchers are actively investigating surface treatment strategies — including the application of hydrophobic coatings and polymer impregnation — to improve nanowood's moisture resistance without significantly increasing its environmental footprint or reducing its biodegradability.", "The trajectory of nanowood research suggests that many of these challenges are surmountable. As fabrication techniques improve and production scales up, manufacturing costs are expected to fall. Pilot projects integrating nanowood panels into building prototypes have reported promising results in real-world thermal performance testing. If these early findings hold at larger scales, nanowood could represent not merely an incremental improvement over existing insulation technology but a genuinely transformative shift toward construction materials that are simultaneously high-performing, sustainable, and derived from one of the world's oldest and most abundant natural resources."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of this passage?", options: ["The environmental benefits of nanowood are undermined by the deforestation caused by growing demand for balsa wood", "Nanowood is a promising bio-based material with exceptional thermal and mechanical properties that could transform sustainable insulation", "Nanowood is too expensive and difficult to produce to ever replace conventional synthetic insulation materials commercially", "Nanowood's primary advantage over existing insulation materials is its optical property of producing a bright white appearance"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"delignification\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["A mechanical pressing technique that aligns the wood's natural fibres into a uniform directional structure", "A thermal treatment that converts the wood's cellulose into a transparent, heat-resistant polymer film", "A freeze-drying process that compresses the wood's cellular channels into a thin, paper-like sheet", "A chemical process that selectively removes the lignin responsible for wood's brown colour and structural rigidity"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, how does nanowood's thermal conductivity compare to that of expanded polystyrene?", options: ["Nanowood's thermal conductivity is significantly higher than that of polystyrene, making it better suited for warm climates only", "Nanowood and expanded polystyrene have nearly identical thermal conductivity values, making them equivalent in insulating performance", "Nanowood performs better than polystyrene only when heat flows along the grain rather than perpendicular to the cell channels", "Nanowood achieves a significantly lower thermal conductivity than expanded polystyrene, giving it superior insulating performance"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as an additional advantage of nanowood?", options: ["An ability to conduct electricity efficiently, making it suitable for integration into smart building energy systems", "A high specific strength that allows it to bear structural loads that polystyrene foam cannot support", "An optical property that scatters visible light to produce a bright white appearance with potential coating applications", "Full biodegradability that avoids the microplastic and toxic gas pollution linked to synthetic insulation disposal"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what sustainability concern is raised about using balsa wood as the primary feedstock for nanowood?", options: ["The transportation of balsa wood from South America to research facilities generates significant carbon emissions", "Balsa wood is too rare and slow-growing to be harvested at the scale required for industrial nanowood production", "Increasing commercial demand for balsa wood, primarily grown in Ecuador, has been linked to deforestation concerns in some regions", "Balsa wood contains higher levels of lignin than other species, requiring more chemicals to process and generating more waste"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, why are the chemicals used in nanowood production considered environmentally responsible?", options: ["They require no energy to produce and can be synthesized directly from waste products of the timber industry", "They are derived entirely from plant-based sources and break down harmlessly in soil and water after use", "They are present in such small quantities that they have no measurable impact on local water or air quality", "They are recyclable within a closed-loop industrial system, reducing the waste and chemical pollution of manufacturing"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"Its\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["Nanowood itself, the lightweight bio-based material derived from natural wood described throughout the passage", "The polystyrene foam that nanowood is intended to replace as the dominant commercial insulation material", "The specific strength ratio of nanowood that rivals engineering plastics and metals in mechanical performance", "The aligned cellular architecture of balsa wood that gives nanowood its directional insulating properties"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"anisotropic\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Progressively degrading in performance over time as repeated exposure to heat weakens the material's structure", "Having different properties or performance characteristics depending on the direction in which they are measured", "Extremely sensitive to changes in environmental conditions such as humidity, temperature, and atmospheric pressure", "Uniformly consistent in all directions, delivering the same level of performance regardless of orientation"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what are the two main practical challenges facing nanowood on the path to commercial adoption?", options: ["Limited availability of suitable wood species and the difficulty of recycling nanowood at the end of its useful life", "Incompatibility with standard building construction methods and the material's tendency to shrink under thermal stress", "The energy-intensive and costly freeze-drying production step, and the material's sensitivity to moisture and humidity", "Public unfamiliarity with bio-based materials and the lack of regulatory standards for nanowood in construction codes"], answer: 2 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'This directionality, while a potential limitation in certain applications, can also be exploited as an advantage in building designs that require selective heat channelling or cooling.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "Drivers of National Development: Why Some Countries Prosper and Others Struggle", paragraphs: ["The question of why some nations achieve sustained economic growth and rising living standards while others remain trapped in cycles of poverty and stagnation has occupied economists, political scientists, and development theorists for generations. No single factor has proven universally sufficient to explain the divergence in national outcomes observed across the world. Instead, research increasingly points to a complex interplay of institutional quality, geographical conditions, historical legacies, human capital investment, and macroeconomic policy as the key determinants of a country's developmental trajectory. Understanding how these factors interact — and how policy can influence them — remains one of the most consequential challenges in the social sciences.", "Institutions — the formal rules, legal systems, and governance structures that shape how societies organize economic and political life — are widely regarded as among the most powerful predictors of long-run development. The influential work of economists Daron Acemoglu and James Robinson, most accessibly presented in their 2012 book Why Nations Fail , argues that the critical distinction lies between \"inclusive\" institutions, which distribute economic and political power broadly across society and create incentives for innovation and investment, and \"extractive\" institutions, which concentrate power among a narrow elite and are designed primarily to extract resources from the wider population. Countries whose institutional frameworks evolved toward inclusivity — such as those of Western Europe and East Asia — have generally achieved higher and more stable long-run growth rates than those where extractive arrangements became entrenched. These patterns are not immutable, however: institutional change, while difficult and often slow, is possible and has been documented in numerous historical cases.", "Geography and natural endowments constitute a second major cluster of developmental factors, though their role is more contested than that of institutions. Economist Jeffrey Sachs has argued that landlocked countries, tropical climates, and disease-prone environments impose significant structural disadvantages on development by raising the costs of trade, reducing agricultural productivity, and diverting scarce public resources toward combating endemic illness. [1] The famous \"resource curse\" — the paradox whereby countries richly endowed with natural resources such as oil, diamonds, or minerals sometimes experience slower growth and worse governance than resource-poor nations — illustrates how geography can interact with institutions in complex and often counterproductive ways. [2] Countries that discover large resource deposits often see state revenues captured by narrow elites, reducing the pressure on governments to develop efficient tax systems and accountable governance. [3] However, counterexamples such as Botswana and Norway demonstrate that natural resource wealth can support rather than undermine development when embedded within strong institutional and governance frameworks. [4]", "Human capital — the accumulated stock of education, skills, health, and knowledge within a population — is consistently identified in empirical research as one of the most robust drivers of economic development. Countries that invest heavily in universal primary and secondary education, accessible healthcare, and vocational training tend to develop more diversified, productive, and resilient economies than those that neglect these foundations. The East Asian developmental experience — including the remarkable growth trajectories of South Korea, Taiwan, Singapore, and more recently China — is frequently cited as evidence of the transformative power of sustained human capital investment, particularly when combined with export-oriented industrial policies and macroeconomic stability. Critics of pure human capital theories, however, note that education alone does not guarantee development if graduates lack productive employment opportunities, a problem sometimes described as \"brain drain\" when talented individuals emigrate to higher-opportunity economies abroad.", "Macroeconomic policy and integration into global trade networks represent a further set of decisive developmental influences. Stable inflation, prudent fiscal management, and competitive exchange rates create an environment in which private investment can flourish and businesses can plan for the long term. Access to international markets amplifies the benefits of specialization, allowing countries to generate income from goods and services in which they hold comparative advantage. However, the relationship between trade openness and development is nuanced : poorly managed trade liberalization has in some cases undermined domestic industries before they were capable of competing internationally, and the distributional consequences of globalization — in terms of which segments of society benefit and which are left behind — continue to generate significant political controversy both within and between nations.", "Cultural and social factors, while harder to measure and more sensitive to discuss, also appear to influence developmental outcomes in ways that purely economic frameworks may underestimate. Trust between citizens and between citizens and government institutions has been linked to lower transaction costs, higher rates of civic participation, and greater political stability — all of which facilitate sustained development. Demographic dynamics, including fertility rates, age structures, and urbanization patterns, interact with economic policy in ways that can either accelerate or retard growth depending on the institutional and infrastructural capacity to absorb expanding populations or ageing workforces. A comprehensive theory of national development must therefore accommodate not only economic variables but also the social and political environments within which economic activity takes place.", "What emerges from this literature is not a simple checklist of development prerequisites but a recognition that development is path-dependent, context-specific, and deeply shaped by the accidents of history and geography as well as by deliberate policy choices. For policymakers, the implication is not despair but a call for institutional humility: effective development strategies must be adapted to local conditions rather than imported wholesale from success stories elsewhere, and must address the interacting constellation of factors — institutional, human, geographical, and social — that together determine whether a nation's potential can be translated into sustained and equitable prosperity."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of this passage?", options: ["Institutional quality is the single most decisive factor in national development, overshadowing all geographical and cultural influences", "National development is shaped by a complex interplay of institutional, geographical, human capital, and social factors with no single universal explanation", "The East Asian development model should be adopted by all developing nations as the most reliable path to sustained economic growth", "Geography and natural resource endowments are the primary barriers to development in tropical and landlocked countries worldwide"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"entrenched\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Gradually weakened by the pressures of globalization and the demands of international trade agreements", "Publicly challenged by reform movements that seek to redistribute political and economic power across society", "Firmly and deeply established in a way that makes change difficult, having become embedded over a long period", "Recently introduced through deliberate policy reform in response to a period of economic or political crisis"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what distinguishes \"inclusive\" institutions from \"extractive\" ones, according to Acemoglu and Robinson?", options: ["Inclusive institutions are found exclusively in wealthy nations, while extractive institutions are characteristic of low-income economies", "Inclusive institutions prioritize economic growth over political participation, while extractive institutions do the opposite", "Inclusive institutions distribute power broadly and incentivize innovation, while extractive ones concentrate power to exploit the wider population", "Inclusive institutions are imposed by international organizations, while extractive institutions develop organically within a society"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned by Jeffrey Sachs as a geographical disadvantage to development?", options: ["Experiencing frequent natural disasters that destroy infrastructure and displace agricultural communities", "Having a tropical climate that reduces agricultural productivity compared to temperate regions", "Being a landlocked country, which raises the costs of international trade and market access", "Facing disease-prone environments that divert public resources toward combating endemic illness"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is the \"resource curse\"?", options: ["The tendency for countries with abundant natural resources to over-invest in extraction at the expense of manufacturing industries", "The economic instability caused by heavy dependence on commodity exports whose prices fluctuate on global markets", "The paradox in which countries richly endowed with natural resources sometimes experience slower growth and worse governance than resource-poor ones", "The environmental degradation that occurs when natural resource extraction is prioritized over sustainable land management"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does the East Asian development experience demonstrate?", options: ["That democratic governance is a necessary precondition for the successful implementation of human capital investment programs", "That export-oriented industrial policy alone is sufficient to generate sustained economic growth without investment in education", "That sustained investment in human capital, combined with export-oriented policies and macroeconomic stability, can produce transformative growth", "That South Korea, Taiwan, and Singapore achieved development primarily through foreign direct investment rather than domestic policy"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"These\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["The economists and political scientists who have studied the relationship between institutions and development outcomes", "The inclusive institutional frameworks that Western European and East Asian countries developed over their histories", "The extractive institutional arrangements that became concentrated in a narrow elite in many developing nations", "The patterns of correlation between institutional type and long-run growth rates observed across different countries"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"nuanced\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Primarily relevant to advanced economies and of limited applicability to the development challenges of poorer nations", "Straightforward and well-documented, supported by extensive empirical evidence from across all regions of the world", "Complex and subtle, involving important distinctions and qualifications that prevent simple or one-sided conclusions", "Hotly disputed among economists, with no consensus emerging from decades of academic research and policy debate"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, how does social trust contribute to national development?", options: ["It lowers transaction costs, increases civic participation, and contributes to greater political stability that supports sustained development", "It promotes higher birth rates, ensuring that growing economies have a sufficient workforce to sustain long-term production", "It enables governments to implement unpopular economic reforms without facing significant public opposition or political instability", "It encourages citizens to accept lower wages in exchange for greater job security, reducing labor costs for domestic industries"], answer: 0 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'Countries that discover large resource deposits often see state revenues captured by narrow elites, reducing the pressure on governments to develop efficient tax systems and accountable governance.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 2 }] },
    { title: "Climate Change: Scientific Consensus, Policy Fractures, and the Politics of Inaction", paragraphs: ["Few issues in contemporary public life illustrate the gap between scientific knowledge and political action as starkly as climate change. The scientific consensus on anthropogenic global warming is, by any reasonable measure, overwhelming: the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change (IPCC), which synthesizes the work of thousands of researchers across disciplines, has concluded in successive assessment reports that human activities — primarily the burning of fossil fuels, deforestation, and industrial agriculture — are unambiguously driving unprecedented increases in global average temperatures, sea levels, and the frequency and severity of extreme weather events. Yet despite this scientific clarity, global greenhouse gas emissions have continued to rise, international climate agreements have fallen consistently short of the reductions deemed necessary to avoid the most catastrophic warming scenarios, and public discourse on climate change remains fractured along political, economic, and ideological lines in ways that have profoundly limited effective collective action. [1]", "The architecture of international climate governance has evolved significantly since the adoption of the United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change (UNFCCC) in 1992. The 1997 Kyoto Protocol established binding emissions reduction targets for developed nations, but its exclusion of major developing emitters such as China and India, combined with the United States' failure to ratify the agreement, significantly undermined its effectiveness. [2] The 2015 Paris Agreement represented a structural departure from the Kyoto model: rather than imposing binding targets from above, it invited countries to submit their own Nationally Determined Contributions (NDCs) — voluntary pledges to reduce emissions according to each nation's circumstances and capabilities. This design reflected a pragmatic recognition that universal binding commitments were politically unachievable, but critics argue that the voluntary architecture of Paris creates a fundamental collective action problem, since individual nations face strong short-term incentives to free-ride on others' mitigation efforts rather than bearing the domestic economic costs of deep decarbonization.", "The asymmetry between those who have contributed most to historical emissions and those who face the gravest consequences of warming is one of the most contentious fault lines in climate politics. Wealthy industrialized nations — primarily in North America, Europe, and Japan — are responsible for the overwhelming majority of the cumulative greenhouse gas emissions that have accumulated in the atmosphere since the Industrial Revolution. Yet the populations most acutely threatened by climate impacts — including coastal communities in South and Southeast Asia, subsistence farmers in sub-Saharan Africa, and small island developing states in the Pacific — have contributed negligibly to the problem. This inequity has generated persistent demands from developing nations for \"climate justice\": the principle that wealthy nations should bear a disproportionate share of the mitigation and adaptation costs, and should provide substantial financial and technological support to less wealthy countries transitioning to low-carbon development pathways.", "The economic dimensions of climate policy have proven particularly resistant to consensus. Proponents of aggressive mitigation argue that the costs of inaction — measured in terms of reduced agricultural yields, increased healthcare burdens, infrastructure damage from extreme weather, and potential civilizational disruption — dwarf the investments required for a rapid transition to renewable energy. [3] The influential Stern Review of 2006, commissioned by the UK government, estimated that the costs of unmitigated climate change could reach the equivalent of losing between five and twenty percent of global GDP permanently, while the costs of stabilizing atmospheric greenhouse gas concentrations at safe levels amounted to approximately one percent of annual global GDP. Opponents of aggressive mitigation, however, dispute both the severity of projected damages and the feasibility of the transition, raising concerns about the effects of carbon pricing on industrial competitiveness, energy affordability for low-income households, and the pace of technological innovation needed to replace fossil fuels without disrupting economic growth.", "The role of vested interests in shaping climate policy — and particularly in generating and amplifying public uncertainty about climate science — has attracted substantial scholarly and journalistic attention. Investigative research has documented that major fossil fuel corporations, including ExxonMobil and Shell, possessed internal scientific assessments as early as the 1970s and 1980s that accurately projected the scale of anthropogenic warming, yet simultaneously funded public relations campaigns and think tanks that sowed doubt about the scientific consensus among policymakers and the general public. This strategy of manufactured uncertainty, drawing on techniques pioneered by the tobacco industry's campaigns against evidence linking smoking to lung cancer, has been credited by some researchers with substantially delaying meaningful climate legislation in the United States and other major emitting nations.", "Technological optimism offers a counterweight to pessimistic assessments of the political feasibility of deep decarbonization. The cost of solar photovoltaic technology has fallen by more than ninety percent since 2010, wind energy has become cost-competitive with fossil fuels in most major markets, and battery storage costs are declining rapidly in ways that are expanding the viability of electrified transportation and grid-scale renewable energy storage. Some analysts argue that the pace of clean energy deployment has outrun the predictions of even optimistic models, suggesting that the energy transition may be driven as much by market forces and technological learning curves as by regulatory compulsion. Carbon capture and storage technologies, while still expensive and unproven at scale, represent a further potential tool for drawing down atmospheric carbon dioxide concentrations if deployment challenges can be overcome.", "What the climate debate ultimately reveals is a collision between the logic of science — which operates on timescales of decades and centuries, demands systemic solutions, and brooks no negotiation with physical reality — and the logic of democratic politics, which operates on electoral cycles of four to five years, responds to concentrated short-term interests, and must navigate the distributional conflicts that any major economic transformation inevitably generates. [4] Whether democratic institutions prove capable of responding to a challenge of this temporal scale and complexity, or whether the window for effective action will close before sufficient political will can be mobilized, remains the defining uncertainty of the twenty-first century."], questions: [{ q: "What is the primary purpose of this passage?", options: ["To argue that the Paris Agreement's voluntary architecture is fundamentally superior to the binding targets established by the Kyoto Protocol", "To analyze the gap between overwhelming scientific consensus on climate change and the persistent failures of political action to address it", "To provide a comprehensive history of international climate negotiations from the 1992 UNFCCC to the present day", "To demonstrate that technological innovation in renewable energy has already resolved the most urgent challenges of climate change"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is a key criticism of the Paris Agreement's voluntary architecture?", options: ["It excludes the largest emitting nations from participation, making its emissions reduction pledges statistically irrelevant", "It creates a collective action problem where individual nations have incentives to free-ride rather than bear decarbonization costs", "It places binding emissions targets on developing nations before they have had the opportunity to industrialize their economies", "It gives international bodies too much authority to override the domestic energy policies of sovereign nation states"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"fractured\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Carefully structured to accommodate diverse viewpoints while preserving a common foundation for negotiation", "Broadly unified around a shared set of policy priorities despite surface differences in national circumstance", "Deeply divided into competing and often incompatible positions along political, economic, or ideological lines", "Temporarily disrupted by short-term political controversies but fundamentally stable in its overall direction"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what did the Stern Review estimate about the economic costs of unmitigated climate change?", options: ["That unmitigated climate change would cost approximately one percent of annual global GDP, the same as the cost of stabilizing emissions", "That unmitigated climate change could permanently reduce global GDP by between five and twenty percent", "That the costs of climate stabilization would exceed the costs of inaction by a ratio of five to one over the next century", "That developing nations would bear ninety percent of the total economic costs while contributing minimally to emissions"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a concern raised by opponents of aggressive climate mitigation?", options: ["The pace of technological innovation needed to replace fossil fuels without disrupting economic growth", "The potential effects of carbon pricing on the international competitiveness of domestic industries", "The possibility that sea-level rise will displace coastal populations faster than adaptation measures can be implemented", "The risk that rapid decarbonization will make energy unaffordable for low-income households"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what strategy did major fossil fuel corporations allegedly use to influence climate policy?", options: ["They funded campaigns that sowed public doubt about the scientific consensus on climate change despite possessing accurate internal projections", "They funded political campaigns to elect candidates who opposed ratification of the Kyoto Protocol and the Paris Agreement", "They lobbied directly for the exclusion of the energy sector from binding emissions reduction targets in international agreements", "They acquired renewable energy companies to delay the commercialization of clean technology and protect their fossil fuel revenues"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what evidence supports technological optimism about the clean energy transition?", options: ["All major economies have committed to phasing out fossil fuel subsidies and redirecting investment into renewable energy by 2030", "Carbon capture technologies have already been deployed at industrial scale, permanently reducing atmospheric CO2 concentrations", "Battery storage technology has already enabled complete electrification of heavy industry and long-haul freight transportation", "Solar photovoltaic costs have fallen over ninety percent since 2010, and wind energy is now cost-competitive with fossil fuels"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"sowed\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Systematically and deliberately spread or planted something — in this context, uncertainty — in people's minds", "Gradually eroded public trust in government institutions responsible for regulating fossil fuel industry activities", "Carefully concealed or suppressed inconvenient scientific evidence from public view through legal and financial means", "Openly challenged and publicly refuted the findings of independent scientific researchers through peer-reviewed publications"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"This\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["The failure of the United States to ratify the Kyoto Protocol, which significantly undermined its global effectiveness", "The voluntary architecture of the Paris Agreement in which countries submit their own Nationally Determined Contributions", "The structural departure from previous climate agreements represented by the Paris Agreement's bottom-up approach", "The binding emissions reduction framework established by the Kyoto Protocol for industrialized nations in 1997"], answer: 1 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'This disconnect — between what the science demands and what politics has delivered — is the central tension that every subsequent section of this analysis will attempt to illuminate.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 0 }] },
  ] },
  { id: "rd-165", num: 165, title: "Đề 165", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "A Musical Tribute to the Sailors of the Islands", paragraphs: ["Each year, a handful of cultural programs attempt to bring the lives of soldiers stationed on Vietnam's remote island outposts to audiences who will never set foot there themselves, but few have managed to do so with the emotional reach of a musical program first staged three years ago and now touring several provinces annually. Built around a cycle of original songs rather than a single narrative arc, the program was conceived not as documentary reportage but as an attempt to translate distance and longing into something an urban audience could feel rather than merely learn about secondhand.", "The program's musical direction rests largely with composer Dinh Bao, who spent several weeks aboard supply vessels traveling between mainland ports and the outposts before writing a single note. He has said in interviews that the songs were shaped less by what soldiers told him directly than by what he noticed they left unsaid — long pauses before answering questions about home, the way letters were reread until the paper softened. That reluctance to narrate their own hardship directly, he has suggested, told him more about the emotional weight of the posting than any complaint could have.", "Life at the outposts, as depicted across the program's dozen musical numbers, is defined less by danger in the conventional sense than by the accumulation of small deprivations: fresh vegetables that arrive infrequently, phone signals that vanish for weeks during storm season, birthdays and anniversaries marked alone rather than with family. According to figures cited in the program's accompanying materials, several hundred servicemen currently rotate through these postings at any given time, a number that has stayed relatively steady even as the vessels supplying them have gradually improved. It is this quieter, cumulative form of hardship, rather than any single dramatic event, that the songs return to again and again.", "Structurally, the program alternates between full-ensemble numbers depicting collective military routine and quieter solo pieces built around a single soldier's imagined letter home, a device that lets the production shift registers without losing narrative coherence. Between musical numbers, brief spoken interludes performed by actors reading from real letters — with names changed at the families' request — anchor the more stylized songs in testimony that audiences can trust as authentic, even where the melodies themselves are entirely invented.", "Reception has been notably warmer among audiences who initially expected a straightforwardly patriotic tribute and instead encountered something quieter and more personal; several reviewers have remarked that the program earns its emotional impact precisely by resisting the temptation toward overt sentimentality in its opening numbers, saving its most affecting moments for the second half instead. Ticket demand has grown steadily enough that a fourth touring cycle, extending the program to two additional provinces, is already being planned for next year.", "Asked what he most wants audiences to carry away from the program, Dinh Bao has repeatedly returned to a single idea: not sympathy for the soldiers as distant figures deserving pity, but a felt recognition of the ordinary, unglamorous persistence their daily routine actually requires, the sense that endurance itself, sustained quietly over months without applause, is the story worth telling."], questions: [{ q: "Which of the following titles would best fit the passage as a whole?", options: ["A Musical Tribute to the Sailors of the Islands", "How Supply Ships Reach Remote Outposts", "A Review of This Year's Best Theater Productions", "The History of Vietnamese Military Music"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which paragraph provides a figure for the number of servicemen currently rotating through the outposts?", options: ["Paragraph 5, which describes audience reception of the program", "Paragraph 3, which describes the accumulation of small deprivations at the outposts", "Paragraph 2, which describes the composer's time aboard supply vessels", "Paragraph 1, which introduces the program's musical structure"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"That\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["the interview format Dinh Bao used with soldiers", "the softened paper of reread letters", "the composer's decision to travel by supply vessel", "the soldiers' reluctance to narrate their own hardship directly"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT true of the musical program as described in paragraph 1?", options: ["It is built around a cycle of original songs rather than a single narrative arc", "It was conceived as documentary reportage rather than an emotional translation of experience", "It is now touring several provinces annually", "It was first staged three years before the time of writing"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"reluctance\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["unwillingness", "confusion", "curiosity", "eagerness"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what kind of hardship do the program's songs return to most often?", options: ["The physical danger of storms damaging the supply vessels", "The quieter, cumulative form of hardship built from small daily deprivations", "Conflicts between soldiers stationed at different outposts", "A single dramatic and life-threatening event experienced by the soldiers"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"anchor\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["confuse", "ground", "replace", "postpone"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What can be inferred about why Dinh Bao paid close attention to what soldiers left unsaid rather than what they told him directly?", options: ["Their silences and habits revealed the posting's true emotional weight.", "Because he was primarily interested in documenting the technical details of outpost life", "Because the soldiers refused to speak with him at all during his time aboard the supply vessels", "Because military regulations prohibited soldiers from discussing their daily routines with visitors"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, why has audience reception been notably warm?", options: ["Because the program features well-known professional soldiers as its lead performers", "Because ticket prices were lowered to attract a wider range of audiences", "Because the program delivers a straightforwardly patriotic tribute exactly as most audiences expected", "It avoids early sentimentality, saving emotional impact for later."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does Dinh Bao most want audiences to take away from the program?", options: ["Admiration for the composer's musical technique rather than the soldiers themselves", "A felt recognition of the quiet persistence required in their routine", "A sense of pity for soldiers as distant figures deserving sympathy", "An understanding of the technical logistics behind supplying remote outposts"], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "Charting Vietnam's Path Toward Homegrown AI Talent", paragraphs: ["Conversations about artificial intelligence in Vietnam have, over the past several years, shifted noticeably in tone, moving from a near-exclusive focus on adopting foreign AI tools toward a growing, though still contested , belief that Vietnam might eventually develop AI systems of genuine international significance rather than remaining solely a consumer of technology built elsewhere.", "Among the more prominent voices advancing this view is Dean, who leads the Faculty of Information Technology at one of Hanoi's larger technical universities and has, in recent interviews, argued that the country's long-standing strength in mathematics competitions, reflected in consistently strong results at international olympiads over several decades, translates more directly into AI research capability than is commonly assumed, given how heavily modern machine learning depends on exactly the kind of abstract mathematical reasoning those competitions reward.", "Huy's confidence rests on more than competition results alone, however. He points to the rapid expansion of domestic programming communities and open-source contributions from Vietnamese developers over the past decade, alongside a marked increase in government funding directed at university AI research labs, as evidence that the underlying talent pipeline is not merely theoretical but is already producing measurable, if still early-stage, output. It is this combination — mathematical foundation, growing practical coding culture, and rising institutional investment — that he cites most often when asked to justify his optimism.", "Skeptics within the same academic community are not dismissive of Vietnam's mathematical tradition but argue instead that translating competition mathematics into deployable AI research requires infrastructure, most notably computing power and large curated datasets, that Vietnam still lacks relative to countries where major AI labs are concentrated. Without access to comparable computational resources, these critics contend, even exceptionally talented researchers may find themselves conducting smaller-scale studies rather than the large-model research that currently defines the field's cutting edge.", "A separate complication concerns retention rather than raw talent. Vietnamese universities have, for years, produced graduates who go on to build strong reputations at international technology companies, but a persistent pattern of skilled researchers relocating abroad for better-resourced positions has left domestic labs thinner than their graduate output alone would suggest. Some industry figures argue that closing this gap depends less on training more talent than on creating research conditions competitive enough to keep them in the country once trained.", "Huy has acknowledged this retention concern directly but frames it somewhat differently, suggesting that a growing number of overseas-trained Vietnamese researchers are now beginning to return, drawn by newly funded domestic labs and a startup ecosystem that, while still modest by regional standards, has begun offering opportunities that did not exist even five years ago. However, building enough opportunities to fully integrate them into these institutions, he concedes, remains genuinely uncertain rather than something he is prepared to guarantee.", "What emerges from these overlapping perspectives is less a settled prediction than an open question shaped by competing pressures: strong mathematical foundations and rising investment pulling in one direction, infrastructure gaps and talent retention pulling in the other, with the eventual balance likely to depend on decisions — about funding, infrastructure, and researcher retention — that have not yet been fully made."], questions: [{ q: "Which of the following titles would best fit the passage as a whole?", options: ["The History of Vietnam's University System", "Charting Vietnam's Path Toward Homegrown AI Talent", "How Vietnamese Students Win International Math Olympiads", "A Guide to Studying Abroad in Computer Science"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, who is Dean?", options: ["Head of IT Faculty at a major Hanoi university", "A skeptical researcher who doubts Vietnam's mathematical tradition", "A government official responsible for national technology funding decisions", "An overseas-trained researcher who recently returned to found a startup"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what evidence does Dean cite to support his belief that Vietnamese students can develop AI?", options: ["Government funding increases alone, without reference to programming communities or mathematical tradition", "The number of Vietnamese researchers who have already relocated abroad to join major AI labs", "Math strengths, growing coding communities, and rising state funding", "Vietnam's strong olympiad mathematics results alone, without reference to any other factor"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What can be inferred about why skeptics believe Vietnam's mathematical talent may not translate directly into cutting-edge AI research?", options: ["Because talented researchers lack sufficient interest in pursuing AI as a career path", "Because international math olympiads no longer reflect genuine mathematical ability", "Lack of computing power and datasets limits work to smaller studies", "Because Vietnam's universities do not currently teach advanced mathematics"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a factor in the debate over Vietnam's AI development?", options: ["A shortage of computing power and large curated datasets", "A national law restricting the export of Vietnamese AI software abroad", "Vietnam's mathematical tradition reflected in olympiad results", "The relocation of skilled researchers to better-resourced positions overseas"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"contested\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["confirmed", "disputed", "forgotten", "irrelevant"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"thinner\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["less staffed", "better funded", "more competitive", "more advanced"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"them\" in paragraph 5 refers to _______.", options: ["skilled researchers", "domestic universities", "government officials", "international companies"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"them\" in paragraph 6 refers to _______.", options: ["overseas-trained Vietnamese researchers", "startup ecosystem investors", "domestic university labs", "foreign AI companies"], answer: 0 }, { q: "What is the author's overall attitude toward the question of whether Vietnam will develop internationally significant AI capability?", options: ["Firmly convinced that Vietnam's mathematical talent guarantees future AI success", "Strongly critical of Dean's optimism as unsupported by any evidence", "Dismissive of the entire debate as lacking any credible evidence on either side", "Balanced and uncertain, highlighting pressures from both sides"], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "Reassessing Einstein's Path to Scientific Achievement", paragraphs: ["That Albert Einstein would come to symbolize scientific genius itself is, in one sense, an unlikely outcome given how unremarkable his early academic trajectory actually was; he struggled to secure a university teaching post after graduation, and it was only through a former classmate's father that he obtained a position evaluating patent applications in Bern, a job most historians now agree was less a detour from his scientific development than an unusual accelerant of it. The patent office required him to assess the technical feasibility of proposed inventions quickly and rigorously, a discipline that, several biographers argue, sharpened precisely the kind of conceptual clarity his subsequent physics would demand.", "It was during this period, working outside any formal academic research institution, that Einstein produced four papers in 1905 now referred to collectively as his \"miracle year\" output, though the phrase itself somewhat flattens how differently each paper was received at the time. The paper explaining the photoelectric effect, which proposed that light itself could be understood as discrete packets of energy rather than a continuous wave, was initially met with skepticism even among physicists sympathetic to Einstein's other work, precisely because it challenged the wave theory of light that decades of experimental support had seemingly confirmed.", "The special theory of relativity, published the same year, addressed a separate problem entirely: reconciling the behavior of light with the principle that physical laws should hold consistently regardless of an observer's motion. [1] Unlike the photoelectric paper, special relativity required no new experimental apparatus to propose, relying instead on thought experiments and mathematical reasoning, [2] a methodological difference that some contemporaries found either elegantly minimalist or suspiciously unverifiable, [3] depending on their existing disposition toward theoretical physics. [4]", "A full decade passed before Einstein extended these ideas into general relativity, published in 1915, which reframed gravity not as a force acting across space but as a consequence of mass curving space-time itself. The theory's confirmation came only in 1919, when astronomer Arthur Eddington's observations of starlight bending during a solar eclipse matched Einstein's predicted values closely enough to convert much of the remaining scientific skepticism, transforming Einstein almost overnight from a respected physicist into an internationally recognized public figure.", "It is a detail frequently misremembered that Einstein's 1921 Nobel Prize was awarded specifically for general relativity; the Nobel committee's citation instead credited his earlier work on the photoelectric effect, a decision that reflected the committee's persistent unease about relativity's still-unresolved experimental status at the time rather than any judgment that the photoelectric paper was his more significant contribution. This distinction, though minor to a casual reader, illustrates how contested even the most celebrated of his ideas remained among specialists years after publication.", "Einstein's later career, following his emigration to the United States in 1933 amid rising political persecution in Germany, diverged in a direction not fully anticipated by his earlier scientific output: rather than continuing to produce work with comparable impact, he devoted considerable energy to a decades-long, ultimately unsuccessful search for a unified field theory that would reconcile gravity with electromagnetism, a pursuit several colleagues at the time regarded as increasingly disconnected from where mainstream physics, particularly quantum mechanics, was heading.", "His public role during this period extended well beyond physics; he became an outspoken advocate for civil rights and nuclear disarmament, positions that some biographers suggest were shaped as much by his experience of displacement and persecution as by any strictly scientific reasoning, complicating any narrative that treats his later activism as separate from, rather than continuous with, the convictions formed earlier in his life.", "Taken together, Einstein's career resists the tidy arc often assigned to it in popular retellings: a supposed steady ascent from obscure patent clerk to celebrated genius understates how contested his early ideas were among peers, how narrowly the 1921 Nobel citation avoided his most famous theory, and how uncertain even his own later research proved relative to the trajectory of physics as a field."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea conveyed by the passage as a whole?", options: ["Einstein's career was more complex, contested, and uncertain than popular myths suggest.", "Einstein's scientific reputation rests primarily on his political activism rather than on his physics research", "Einstein's career followed a steady, uninterrupted rise from patent clerk to celebrated genius, exactly as popular retellings suggest", "Einstein's most significant contribution to physics was his decades-long search for a unified field theory late in his career"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, how do most historians now regard Einstein's time at the patent office?", options: ["As a role entirely unrelated to the conceptual clarity of his later physics", "As a wasted period that delayed his genuine scientific development by several years", "As a position he held only briefly before immediately obtaining an academic post", "As an unusual accelerant of his scientific development rather than a mere detour from it"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The phrase \"a methodological difference\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["the reliance on thought experiments over new laboratory equipment", "the disagreement between Einstein and Eddington over eclipse observations", "the difference between special and general relativity in their year of publication", "the difference between Einstein's early and later research on unified field theory"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"disposition\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["employment", "location", "inclination", "publication"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as true regarding Einstein's 1921 Nobel Prize?", options: ["The Nobel committee's citation credited his work on the photoelectric effect", "The committee harbored persistent unease about relativity's unresolved experimental status", "It is frequently misremembered as having been awarded for general relativity", "The prize was awarded jointly to Einstein and Arthur Eddington for their combined work"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what event in 1919 helped convert much of the remaining scientific skepticism toward general relativity?", options: ["Einstein's emigration to the United States following political persecution in Germany", "Arthur Eddington's eclipse observations of starlight bending, which closely matched Einstein's predicted values", "The publication of Einstein's paper on the photoelectric effect", "The Nobel committee's decision to award Einstein the physics prize that year"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What can be inferred about why the 1921 Nobel citation for the photoelectric effect, rather than relativity, is described as a frequently misremembered detail?", options: ["The Nobel committee later retracted the prize once relativity was confirmed", "It highlights that his famous theory remained controversial for years.", "Einstein himself publicly disputed the committee's decision at the time", "It proves that the photoelectric effect was scientifically more significant than general relativity"], answer: 1 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'This absence of experimental grounding was, for some, precisely the theory's appeal rather than its weakness.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What can be inferred about the relationship between Einstein's experience of displacement and his later civil rights activism?", options: ["Biographers agree unanimously that his activism had no connection whatsoever to his personal experiences", "His activism began only after he had fully abandoned his search for a unified field theory", "His personal experience of persecution likely helped shape his activism.", "His experience of displacement led him to abandon political engagement entirely in favor of physics research"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What is the author's overall attitude toward the popular narrative of Einstein's career?", options: ["Focused exclusively on praising Einstein's political activism over his physics", "Dismissive of Einstein's scientific contributions as overstated by history", "Fully accepting, treating the popular steady-ascent narrative as an accurate summary of his life", "Critical, contending that popular myths ignore his career's true complexity."], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "The Uncertain Reach of Prisoners' Legal Protections", paragraphs: ["International frameworks governing the treatment of incarcerated people, most notably the United Nations Standard Minimum Rules on the Treatment of Prisoners, commonly known as the Mandela Rules, articulate protections that read, on paper, as comprehensive: prohibitions on torture and prolonged solitary confinement, guarantees of adequate medical care, and requirements for meaningful contact with the outside world. Whether these standards translate into enforceable rights within any given national legal system, however, is a question the framework itself does not resolve, since the Mandela Rules function as non-binding guidance rather than as a treaty carrying direct legal force.", "This gap between articulated standard and enforceable right is not uniform across jurisdictions, and it would be a mistake to treat the challenge as identical everywhere it arises. In some legal systems, domestic courts have incorporated the Mandela Rules' language into constitutional protections against cruel and unusual punishment, effectively converting soft guidance into binding precedent through judicial interpretation rather than legislative action. In others, courts have explicitly declined to treat the Rules as creating any judicially enforceable obligation, reasoning that non-binding international guidance cannot, by itself, generate domestic legal rights absent explicit incorporating legislation.", "Solitary confinement occupies a particularly contested position within this broader uncertainty. [1] A substantial body of psychological research, accumulated over several decades, links prolonged isolation to measurable harm, including heightened rates of anxiety, self-harm, and cognitive impairment, and this evidence has grown difficult for even skeptical courts to dismiss outright. [2] Yet the same research rarely specifies a universally applicable threshold at which isolation becomes harmful, since individual variation in tolerance appears considerable. [3] This gap is one that some correctional authorities have exploited to argue that any given duration of isolation, however extended, cannot be conclusively presumed harmful in the individual case before the court. [4]", "Sentencing reform advocates have, in recent years, increasingly framed prisoners' legal protections not merely as a matter of humane treatment during incarceration but as inseparable from broader questions about sentence length and proportionality, arguing that protections addressing conditions of confinement mean comparatively little if sentences themselves are calibrated with little regard to demonstrated rehabilitation. This framing has proven persuasive to some policymakers, though it has also drawn criticism from those who argue that conflating conditions-based reform with sentencing reform risks diluting political support for either objective by making the overall proposal harder to characterize and defend.", "Recidivism data further complicates any straightforward account of what prisoner protections are meant to achieve. Programs emphasizing education and vocational training during incarceration are associated, in several long-term studies, with meaningfully lower reoffending rates after release, a finding that has been used to argue for expanded protections and services on essentially pragmatic, rather than purely humanitarian, grounds. Critics of this framing counter that justifying humane treatment primarily through its effect on recidivism risks implying, perhaps unintentionally, that protections would be less warranted for populations less likely to benefit measurably in those terms, an implication most reform advocates explicitly reject even as they continue to cite the recidivism data strategically.", "Legal scholars examining this landscape have increasingly converged on a view that the central obstacle is not an absence of articulated standards, which by most accounts already exist in reasonably comprehensive form, but a persistent enforcement gap between what international and domestic frameworks nominally guarantee and what incarcerated individuals can realistically compel through litigation, particularly where courts remain reluctant to second-guess correctional administrators' operational judgments.", "This deference to administrative judgment is not applied uniformly, however; several recent domestic rulings have narrowed it specifically in cases involving documented, repeated failures to provide adequate medical care, suggesting that judicial reluctance to intervene may be softening incrementally in particular categories of harm even as it persists broadly elsewhere, a pattern too recent and too unevenly distributed across jurisdictions to characterize confidently as a durable shift rather than a set of isolated exceptions."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea conveyed by the passage as a whole?", options: ["Solitary confinement has been definitively proven harmful beyond any reasonable dispute in every legal jurisdiction", "Sentencing reform advocates and conditions-based reform advocates have fully united around a single, uncontested policy proposal", "The primary issue is not lacking standards, but inconsistent domestic enforcement across jurisdictions and issues", "The Mandela Rules function as a binding international treaty that all national courts are legally required to enforce directly"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, why do the Mandela Rules not automatically translate into enforceable domestic rights?", options: ["Because they were withdrawn by the United Nations shortly after being drafted", "Because they apply only to pretrial detainees rather than convicted prisoners", "Because no country has ever attempted to incorporate them into domestic law", "Because they serve as non-binding guidance rather than a legally enforceable treaty"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The phrase \"this gap\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["the gap between solitary confinement's psychological effects and its legal recognition", "the split between courts enforcing the Mandela Rules and those rejecting them", "the difference in funding levels between correctional systems in different countries", "the difference between sentencing reform and conditions-based reform advocacy"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"nominally\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["only in a small number of jurisdictions", "in name but not necessarily in practice", "excessively and beyond reasonable limits", "in a manner scientifically proven"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a criticism of framing prisoner protections around recidivism data?", options: ["It risks implying protections would be less warranted for populations less likely to benefit measurably", "It shifts the justification for humane treatment onto pragmatic rather than purely humanitarian grounds", "It has been proven by multiple courts to violate international human rights treaties directly", "It is a framing most reform advocates explicitly reject even as they continue citing the data strategically"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, why do some correctional authorities argue that extended solitary confinement cannot be conclusively presumed harmful in an individual case?", options: ["Because correctional authorities have their own independent research contradicting all existing studies", "Because courts have unanimously ruled that solitary confinement causes no measurable harm", "Because psychological research has found no link whatsoever between isolation and harm", "Because individual tolerance varies greatly and studies lack a universal harm threshold"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What can be inferred about why some critics oppose combining conditions-based reform with sentencing reform into a single advocacy framing?", options: ["Because merging the goals may weaken political support and complicate defense", "Because sentencing reform has already been fully achieved in most jurisdictions", "Because international law explicitly prohibits combining the two types of reform in a single proposal", "Because they believe conditions-based reform is entirely unnecessary once sentencing reform is achieved"], answer: 0 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'This absence of a fixed threshold has made the question harder to resolve through litigation alone.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What can be inferred about why the recent narrowing of judicial deference in medical care cases is not characterized as a durable shift?", options: ["Because courts have since reversed these rulings and restored full deference to correctional administrators", "Because the rulings applied only to solitary confinement cases rather than medical care cases", "Because every jurisdiction has now adopted the same narrowed standard of judicial deference", "Because the trend is too recent and unevenly applied to confirm a lasting change"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What is the author's overall attitude toward the current state of prisoners' legal protections?", options: ["Strongly advocacy-driven, arguing unambiguously in favor of sentencing reform over conditions-based reform", "Dismissive of international standards as entirely irrelevant to domestic legal outcomes", "Confidently optimistic that enforcement gaps have already been largely resolved through recent court rulings", "Balanced and analytical, presenting contrasting pressures and an evolving legal picture"], answer: 3 }] },
  ] },
];
